# A precise version of the "errors independent + truth dependent" theorem

As a slogan, the two conditions are necessary, and they map onto two standard results. Here is a version that can be proved, with the numbers I computed.

## Formalization
Take an item x with a true answer. Agent i is correct with probability p_i(x). Let p̄(x) be the average over agents.

- **"Errors independent"** means agent outputs are *conditionally independent given the item*. Unconditional independence is the wrong requirement.
- **"Truth dependent"** means the agents' answers carry real information about the truth: p̄(x) > ½ for *every* item class, not just on average.

**Proposition (Condorcet's jury theorem plus Hoeffding's inequality; standard results):**
1. If p̄(x) > ½, majority error is at most exp(−2n(p̄−½)²), which falls exponentially in n.
2. If p̄(x) < ½ on a set of items H with probability mass h, the majority is *wrong with probability tending to 1* on H as n grows. The system defect rate then has a floor of about h that no amount of voting removes.
3. Correlation shrinks the effective panel. A rough design-effect estimate is n_eff = n / (1 + (n−1)ρ). For n = 13, ρ = 0.05 gives about 8, and ρ = 0.1 gives about 6.

So six sigma needs the independence condition and a **truth-anchored competence floor**: nearly no items where the typical agent is more likely wrong than right.

## What the numbers say
**Voters needed for under 3.4 DPMO** (independent, exact binomial, my calculation):

| Per-agent accuracy | Voters needed |
|---|---|
| 99% | 7 |
| 95% | 13 |
| 90% | 21 |
| 80% | 45 |
| 70% | 117 |
| 60% | 497 |

Thirteen works only because agents are 95% accurate. Weaker cross-domain accuracy needs far more voters.

**The competence floor** (13 voters, 95% accurate on easy items, 30% on a "hard" fraction):

| Hard-item fraction | Defects per million | ~Sigma |
|---|---|---|
| 1% | 9,377 | 3.9 |
| 0.1% | 939 | 4.6 |
| 0.001% | 10 | 5.8 |
| 0.0001% | 2 | 6.1 |

Six sigma needs the hard fraction near one in a million. That is why "truth dependent" matters more than panel size: majority voting makes a wrong consensus *more* confident, not less.

## Applying it to the cross-domain design
- **Domain LLMs.** For an item from domain A, most of the 13 domain models are outside their expertise, so their accuracy there may be little better than chance. That makes the effective panel much smaller than 13. You need routing and competence weighting, and voting among all domain models can hurt.
- **Anchors (math axioms, constants, physical laws).** These are the strongest truth-dependent anchors, but with caveats:
  - **Exact:** formal mathematics, checkable by a proof checker. Truth is relative to the axioms (Gödel limits what any fixed system can prove).
  - **Empirical:** physical laws apply within a domain of validity, and measured constants carry uncertainty (G is known only to about five digits; general background).
  - **Dimension and conservation checks** (units, Noether-type conserved quantities) catch many wrong answers cheaply.
  - **Contested knowledge** has no anchor, so sigma is undefined there.
- **RAG.** Retrieval helps ground answers but creates a shared source of failure: if all agents read the same wrong document, they fail together. A 0.1% shared retrieval error alone caps the system near 4.6σ. Use independent retrievers and corpora, and check that retrieved facts were used correctly.
- **The Google AI Mode bullets** describe *capabilities*: concept transfer, shared embeddings, retrieval. They aren't reliability guarantees. Shared embedding spaces and a shared retriever can even raise correlation.

## Status of the statement
Stated precisely, the two conditions are **necessary but not sufficient**. You also need correct decomposition and aggregation, and enough verified ground truth to measure the result (about 880,000 labeled items for a 3.4 DPMO claim at 95% confidence). I'd call this a proposition built on known theorems, not a new theorem.

